# Ansible MCP Tools — Use First

This project has two MCP servers providing Ansible tooling. **Always reach for these before running shell commands, ad-hoc ansible CLI, or kubectl.**

## Server: `ansible` (Red Hat ADT)

| Tool | When to use |
|---|---|
| `ansible_lint` | After editing any playbook or role task file |
| `ansible_navigator` | Running playbooks end-to-end |
| `ade_environment_info` | Checking installed collections, Python, Ansible versions |
| `ade_setup_environment` | Setting up or repairing the dev environment |
| `define_and_build_execution_env` | Building execution environment images |

## Server: `ansible-mcp` (Operational)

### Inventory & Facts
| Tool | When to use |
|---|---|
| `inventory-graph` | Get a human-readable view of all groups and hosts |
| `inventory-find-host` | Look up a host's groups and merged variables |
| `inventory-parse` | Full inventory dump including group_vars/host_vars |
| `ansible-gather-facts` | Collect live facts from a host before writing tasks |

### Playbook Lifecycle
| Tool | When to use |
|---|---|
| `validate-playbook` | Syntax-check any playbook before running it |
| `validate-yaml` | Check raw YAML files for parse errors |
| `project-playbooks` | Discover all playbooks in the project |
| `project-run-playbook` | Run a playbook within the registered project context |
| `ansible-test-idempotence` | Run a playbook twice to confirm no changes on second run |
| `ansible-playbook` | Run a playbook directly |

### Roles & Galaxy
| Tool | When to use |
|---|---|
| `create-role-structure` | Scaffold a new role directory |
| `ansible-role` | Execute a role in isolation for testing |
| `galaxy-install` | Install collections/roles from requirements files |
| `galaxy-lock` | Pin installed versions to a lock file |

### Host Operations
| Tool | When to use |
|---|---|
| `ansible-remote-command` | Run ad-hoc commands on a host (prefer a module instead) |
| `ansible-gather-facts` | Inspect live host state |
| `ansible-diagnose-host` | Full health assessment of a target host |
| `ansible-fetch-logs` | Pull and analyze log files remotely |
| `ansible-service-manager` | Check/manage services on a remote host |

### Vault
| Tool | When to use |
|---|---|
| `vault-view` | Inspect encrypted vault files |
| `vault-encrypt` / `vault-decrypt` | Manage vault encryption |
| `vault-rekey` | Rotate vault passwords |

### Diagnostics & Drift
| Tool | When to use |
|---|---|
| `ansible-capture-baseline` | Snapshot system state before a change |
| `ansible-compare-states` | Diff current state against a baseline snapshot |
| `ansible-health-monitor` | Continuous health trend analysis |
| `ansible-security-audit` | Security posture check |
| `ansible-network-matrix` | Verify connectivity between all hosts |
| `ansible-log-hunter` | Correlate logs across multiple sources |

## Server: `sysoperator`

### Ansible (overlapping — prefer `ansible-mcp` equivalents when project context matters)
| Tool | When to use |
|---|---|
| `run_playbook` | Run a playbook (use `project-run-playbook` from ansible-mcp instead when available) |
| `run_ad_hoc` | Ad-hoc command against hosts |
| `list_inventory` | List hosts and groups (use `inventory-graph` from ansible-mcp for richer output) |
| `list_tasks` | **Unique** — dry-list every task a playbook would execute without running it |
| `check_syntax` | Syntax check (use `validate-playbook` from ansible-mcp instead) |
| `vault_encrypt_string` | Encrypt a single string value with Ansible Vault |
| `vault_decrypt_string` | Decrypt a single encrypted string |

### Terraform
| Tool | When to use |
|---|---|
| `terraform` | Run any Terraform command (init, plan, apply, destroy, validate, output) |

### AWS
| Tool | When to use |
|---|---|
| `aws_ec2` | Manage EC2 instances |
| `aws_s3` | Manage S3 buckets and objects |
| `aws_vpc` | Manage VPC networks |
| `aws_iam` | Manage IAM roles and policies |
| `aws_rds` | Manage RDS instances |
| `aws_elb` | Manage Elastic Load Balancers |
| `aws_lambda` | Manage Lambda functions |
| `aws_route53` | Manage Route53 DNS |
| `aws_cloudformation` | Manage CloudFormation stacks |
| `aws_dynamic_inventory` | Generate AWS dynamic inventory |

## Tool Priority (when servers overlap)

When multiple servers offer the same capability, use in this order:
1. `ansible-mcp` — project-aware, uses registered inventory and config
2. `ansible` (Red Hat ADT) — dev tooling, linting, environment management
3. `sysoperator` — use for unique tools (`list_tasks`, `terraform`, AWS) or as fallback

## Required Workflow

For every Ansible change:
1. `validate-playbook` (ansible-mcp) — syntax check before running
2. `ansible_lint` (ansible) — lint the changed file
3. `list_tasks` (sysoperator) — preview what will execute
4. Run the playbook via `project-run-playbook` (ansible-mcp)
5. `ansible-test-idempotence` (ansible-mcp) — confirm no changes on second run


## Mode Scope

These tools and the required workflow apply in **all Cursor modes** — Agent, Plan, Debug,
and Ask. For the full per-mode breakdown of which tools are required vs situational,
see `02--cussorrules-mcp-briefieng-GENERATED.mdc`.